# Design System: Folio3 Parking

## 1. Visual Theme & Atmosphere
A calm, daylight-first operations interface: **density 6/10, variance 3/10, motion 3/10**. Orderly and human, with quiet warmth that keeps staff worklists readable all day and audit information credible at a glance. This is a responsive web app with one shared, compact header and one visual grammar across employee and administrator workspaces; role changes navigation and access, not the shell. Keep sample records canonical and consistent wherever they appear. There is no marketing hero or decorative dashboard banner.

The references shape the working style, not the brand: **Workday** informs orderly white work surfaces and compact administrative lists; **Gusto** informs plain-language actions and restrained warmth; **ADP** informs sober, tabular scanning for audit-heavy information. Folio3 Parking instead uses a distinctive humanist sans pairing and one muted clay action color. Worklists are information-dense but not cramped: clear row hierarchy, restrained separators, and color only where it helps a person act or recognize a state.

## 2. Color Palette & Roles
One neutral family plus one brand accent. State meaning must also be stated in words and/or shape; color is never the only signal.
- **Daylight Canvas** (`#F5F7F6`) — Main page background, quiet and neutral for phone and desktop use.
- **Paper Surface** (`#FFFFFF`) — Inputs and distinct work areas; no glass or ornamental elevation.
- **Graphite Ink** (`#222927`) — Primary text, headings, table values, and high-contrast controls; never pure black.
- **Quiet Slate** (`#596560`) — Supporting descriptions, timestamps, and secondary information.
- **Rule Grey** (`#D8DEDB`) — Hairline table rules, input borders, and structural separation.
- **Muted Clay** (`#A95540`) — Sole accent for primary actions, selected navigation detail, and visible focus treatment. White text on filled accent controls; no decorative wash or glow.
- **Soft Neutral Fill** (`#F1F3F2`) — Subtle row hover and quiet grouped areas, not a second accent.

Keep error and transaction states explicit with concise labels and a consistent small shape marker. Reserve the accent for the most important action or state in a given context. Maintain at least 4.5:1 contrast for normal text and a clearly visible keyboard focus ring.

## 3. Typography Rules
- **Display / headings:** Hanken Grotesk — sturdy humanist letterforms make section hierarchy clear without oversized dashboard headlines. Use 600 weight for page titles and 500–600 for subordinate headings; tracking around `−0.025em`.
- **Body / interface:** Source Sans 3 — open, readable forms support dense worklists and daylight phone use. Use 16px minimum body text, 14px only for nonessential metadata, and `1.45–1.6` line-height. Keep explanatory prose within 65 characters per line.
- **Mono:** IBM Plex Mono — numbers, space codes, record identifiers, and times only; never labels, statuses, or sentences. Use tabular numerals (`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`) for dates, counts, and measured values.
- **Craft:** Small uppercase labels use `0.07em` tracking. Use curly quotation marks, en dashes in ranges, and real ellipses. Keep one deliberate contrast—compact uppercase metadata against sentence-case work text—rather than changing width, weight, and case everywhere.
- **Fallbacks:** `Hanken Grotesk, Arial, sans-serif`; `Source Sans 3, Arial, sans-serif`; `IBM Plex Mono, monospace`. No serif in this software interface.

## 4. Component Stylings
- **Shared header and navigation:** One light header is shared by both verified roles. Keep its height and typography consistent; show role-appropriate navigation as text, with a clean compact menu on narrow screens. Avoid a second role-specific chrome system, oversized brand banner, and icons on every link.
- **Buttons:** Primary actions use Muted Clay with a clear verb and high-contrast text; secondary actions are neutral outlined or text controls. Minimum 44px touch height. Hover changes the surface subtly; active feedback presses to `scale(0.97)` for 90ms. No glow or pulsing CTA.
- **Worklists and tables:** Flat, scan-first rows with clear column labels, tabular figures, and hairline separators. State is conveyed by concise words plus shape, not color alone. On phones, reflow each record into a labeled stack rather than forcing horizontal scrolling.
- **Cards and grouping:** Elevation is not the default. Prefer white working surfaces, spacing, and border rules; use a lightly bordered, 8px-radius group only when it clarifies a real hierarchy. No equal-card dashboard grids.
- **Inputs and forms:** Persistent label above the field, helper text below where needed, and inline error directly beneath the field. Focus uses a 2px Muted Clay outline with a 2px offset; never rely on floating labels or color alone.
- **Status and empty states:** Keep state labels literal and readable, with a small consistent marker. Empty and error states use specific, helpful plain language and an obvious next action only when one is available; do not add illustrations or invented workflow options.
- **Loading:** Layout-matched skeletal rows and fields, not a generic circular spinner. Respect reduced motion and retain final layout dimensions.
- **Icons and imagery:** Use Lucide at a consistent 1.75px stroke, only where an icon speeds recognition. No decorative stock photography; do not introduce imagery the product data does not provide.

## 5. Layout Principles
Use a 12-column desktop grid inside a centered `max-width: 1440px` frame, with content aligned to the grid rather than randomly offset. The shared header remains the single navigation anchor. Dense information belongs in aligned worklists and compact filter/control rows, with generous separation between sections—not in floating cards. Keep primary task content left aligned and avoid oversized headings.

Build mobile-first with CSS Grid and intrinsic sizing, never percentage `calc()` hacks. Below 768px, collapse multi-column work areas to one column, expose navigation through a simple menu, and reflow tables into labeled records. Prevent horizontal overflow at 375px, 768px, 1024px, and 1440px. Every interactive target is at least 44px; body text stays at least 16px. Use `min-height: 100dvh` where a full viewport is needed, never `100vh` as a fixed-height screen. Maintain clear, non-overlapping spatial zones at every width.

A small, paired “parking-bay” rule may mark a selected parking assignment: two short hairlines beside its identifier, a restrained reference to painted stall markings. Keep this detail rare and functional, never a repeated decoration.

## 6. Motion & Interaction
Motion is quiet confirmation, not atmosphere. CSS transitions use the shared tokens in §9 and affect only `transform` and `opacity` wherever possible. The interface remains understandable when animation is removed.

- **Entrance:** On navigation, the page heading and first work area settle into place with a small 6px movement and modest opacity change. Stagger a few meaningful elements by 50–60ms; reveal larger collections as a group. Total entrance is under 500ms and content is never kept invisible pending animation.
- **Scroll-linked:** No blanket section fade-ins and no motion on every scroll pass. The weekly utilization graphic gets the one-time, data-specific endpoint motion described in §9; it does not alter labels or measured values.
- **Interaction:** Hover gives a row a soft neutral tint; links may draw an underline from the left; buttons press to `scale(0.97)` at 90ms. All controls have a visible `:focus-visible` treatment. Tapped rows respond immediately with a pressed tint.
- **State transitions:** Tabs move a simple underline rather than snapping; disclosure regions use `grid-template-rows: 0fr → 1fr` over 260ms; inserted or reordered records use a brief opacity and 8px shift. Use a shared-element View Transition for a related detail change where supported, otherwise a 24px fade-and-shift. Do not animate layout dimensions or scroll position.
- **Ambient:** Only a genuinely active entry-check indicator may breathe, using the single slow opacity cycle defined in §9. No other perpetual loops.
- **Reduced motion:** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, remove entrance, chart, and ambient animations; keep content fully visible and state changes to a fade of at most 120ms. No scroll-jacking or smooth-scroll libraries.

## 7. Anti-Patterns (Banned)
- No emojis, pure black, neon colors, outer glows, gradient text, gradient meshes, blobs, scanlines, glitch effects, or glassmorphism.
- No Inter or generic system-font styling as the chosen design voice; no serif in software screens.
- No overlapping content, centered marketing hero, oversized hero banner, or decorative 3D device mockup.
- No three equal cards, near-equal bento tiles, four-KPI-plus-sparklines dashboard, icon-in-rounded-square feature list, or repeated identical section template.
- No generic customer/logo strip, star-rating carousel, fake metrics, round-number claims, placeholder records, or generic sample names. Keep sample records consistent across the shared workspace.
- No vague “Get started” CTA, AI copy clichés, filler scroll prompts, custom cursor, or icons on every row.
- No perpetual animation except the single genuinely active status indicator; no bouncing, wiggling, typewriter hero, repeated fade-up, fly-in, parallax text, or animation that restarts on every scroll pass.
- No horizontal scrolling on phones, hidden focus rings, sub-44px touch targets, or color-only transaction/status meaning.

## 8. Voice & Font Rationale
- **Voice:** clear, considerate, accountable.
- **Display:** Hanken Grotesk — humanist forms make headings dependable and direct rather than generic or display-heavy (Google Fonts fallback: Hanken Grotesk).
- **Body / Mono:** Source Sans 3 keeps work text open at compact sizes; IBM Plex Mono gives identifiers and measurements crisp, tabular precision without turning labels into console language.

## 9. Motion System
- **Tokens:** `--dur-instant: 90ms`; `--dur-quick: 160ms`; `--dur-base: 260ms`; `--dur-slow: 420ms`; `--dur-scene: 800ms`; `--ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`; `--ease-in: cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0)`; `--ease-inout: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1)`; `--ease-spring: linear(0, 0.009, 0.035 2.1%, 0.141, 0.281 6.7%, 0.723 12.9%, 0.938 16.7%, 1.017, 1.077 21.4%, 1.121 24%, 1.149 27%, 1.159, 1.154 33%, 1.051 43.2%, 1.013 49.5%, 0.996 57%, 1.001 72%, 1)`; `--travel: 8px`.
- **Entrance:** The heading and first work area settle with `translateY(6px) → 0`, opacity `0.84 → 1`, 420ms `--ease-out`, 55ms stagger; elements remain legible throughout.
- **Scroll-linked:** No repeated scroll reveals. On first view of weekly utilization, its already-visible bar fill makes a one-time 4% endpoint settle; the numeric value remains fixed and legible.
- **Interaction feedback:** Row hover tints `#F1F3F2` in 160ms; underline draws `scaleX(0) → scaleX(1)` in 160ms from the left; button press uses `scale(0.97)` in 90ms; focus uses a 2px accent outline without animation. Tab indicator moves with `--ease-inout` over 260ms.
- **State transitions:** Disclosure uses `grid-template-rows: 0fr → 1fr` over 260ms `--ease-out`; inserted rows use opacity plus `translateY(8px)` over 160ms. Route changes use a shared image/title View Transition when available, otherwise a 24px fade-and-shift.
- **Ambient (max 1):** A dot beside an actively evaluated entry check changes opacity from `0.76 → 1 → 0.76` on a 7.5s ease-in-out cycle; only while that check is active.
- **Signature moment — Measured endpoint settle:** On first entry to weekly utilization, each bar’s visible fill settles from `scaleX(1.04) → scaleX(1)` over 600ms `--ease-out`, staggered 40ms; measured labels do not move or change.
- **Reduced motion:** Remove entrance, endpoint-settle, and ambient keyframes; keep all content visible, and cap remaining state transitions at 120ms.

```css
:root {
  --dur-instant: 90ms;
  --dur-quick: 160ms;
  --dur-base: 260ms;
  --dur-slow: 420ms;
  --dur-scene: 800ms;
  --ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
  --ease-in: cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0);
  --ease-inout: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1);
  --ease-spring: linear(0, 0.009, 0.035 2.1%, 0.141, 0.281 6.7%, 0.723 12.9%, 0.938 16.7%, 1.017, 1.077 21.4%, 1.121 24%, 1.149 27%, 1.159, 1.154 33%, 1.051 43.2%, 1.013 49.5%, 0.996 57%, 1.001 72%, 1);
  --travel: 8px;
}
@keyframes settle-in {
  from { opacity: .84; transform: translateY(6px); }
  to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
}
@keyframes measured-endpoint {
  from { transform: scaleX(1.04); }
  to { transform: scaleX(1); }
}
@keyframes active-check {
  0%, 100% { opacity: .76; }
  50% { opacity: 1; }
}
.page-intro > * { animation: settle-in var(--dur-slow) var(--ease-out) both; }
.utilization-bar.is-entered { transform-origin: left center; animation: measured-endpoint 600ms var(--ease-out) both; }
.entry-check-live::before { animation: active-check 7.5s var(--ease-inout) infinite; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .page-intro > *, .utilization-bar, .entry-check-live::before {
    animation: none !important; opacity: 1; transform: none;
  }
  *, *::before, *::after { scroll-behavior: auto !important; transition-duration: 120ms !important; }
}
```

## 10. Component Substitutions
- **Marketing hero and repeated CTA bands:** Omitted; this is an operational web app. Start from the shared role-aware header and the current task’s clear heading.
- **Four KPI tiles with sparklines:** Use the requested weekly utilization information in a compact, labeled report treatment; never invent figures or comparisons.
- **Feature-card rows and icon tiles:** Use aligned worklists, clear labels, and a hairline rule only where a distinction is needed.
- **Status lozenges that depend on color:** Use literal status text plus one small shape marker and consistent spacing.
- **Decorative photo/avatar strips:** Omit unless a record itself supplies an image; no stock imagery or logo strip.
- **Floating/glass cards and default shadcn treatment:** Use solid white surfaces, the deliberate 8px radius, fine borders, and the custom visible focus outline.
- **Dense table overflowing on mobile:** Reflow the same record and labels into a stacked, touch-sized layout without horizontal scrolling.

## 11. Human Fingerprint
- **Unexpected decision:** A paired “parking-bay” hairline beside the selected space identifier borrows from painted stall markings, giving the interface one product-specific visual cue without adding decorative car imagery.
- **Radius family / elevation levels / grid:** Soft corners, 8px base radius (nested radius 6px), two elevation levels maximum (prefer none; if needed, one faint shadow only), 12-column desktop grid in a 1440px frame, single-column collapse below 768px.
- **Image treatment / icon set:** No decorative images; source-provided imagery, if present, uses a consistent square crop. Lucide icons use 1.75px strokes and appear only where they improve recognition.
